"""
================================================================================
Kudryavtsev ALT Pipeline for Alaska CALM Sites
================================================================================

This script computes Kudryavtsev (Ku) active layer thickness (ALT) predictions
for every Alaska CALM site-year. The output is a training table containing,
for each (site, year) observation:

  - CALM observed ALT (ground truth)
  - Ku-predicted ALT (physics baseline)
  - Residual = CALM ALT - Ku ALT  (the target for the ML residual model)
  - All climate, soil, and site-property inputs used in the Ku calculation

Pipeline overview
-----------------
For each CALM site:
  1. Look up static site properties (vegetation class, organic layer thickness,
     soil texture, ice content) from a site metadata CSV.
  2. For each year with a CALM observation:
       a. Extract monthly air temperature at the site from SNAP rasters
       b. Compute annual mean and amplitude of air temperature
       c. Extract winter snow water equivalent (SWE) from snow rasters
       d. Apply snow + vegetation corrections to get ground-surface T
       e. Compute soil thermal properties from composition
       f. Run Ku equation -> predicted ALT
  3. Pair with observed CALM ALT, write to output table.

Reference for the physics:
  Anisimov, O. A., Shiklomanov, N. I., & Nelson, F. E. (1997). Global warming
  and active-layer thickness: results from transient general circulation
  models. Global and Planetary Change, 15(3-4), 61-77.

  Sazonova, T. S., & Romanovsky, V. E. (2003). A model for regional-scale
  estimation of temporal and spatial variability of active layer thickness
  and mean annual ground temperatures. Permafrost and Periglacial Processes,
  14(2), 125-139.

This implementation is bit-for-bit consistent with the Permamodel CSDMS
reference (Wang, Jafarov, & Overeem 2020).

Author: Andrew Wilcox / Dalton Highway PIML study
================================================================================

INPUTS REQUIRED (file paths set in the CONFIG section below):

  1. CALM_SITES_CSV
       Columns: site_id, site_name, lat, lon, vegetation_class,
                organic_layer_cm, sand_fraction, porosity,
                water_content, ice_content_class

  2. CALM_OBSERVATIONS_CSV
       Columns: site_id, year, alt_observed_cm
       (Long-format: one row per site-year ALT measurement)

  3. SNAP_TEMP_DIR
       Folder containing monthly air temperature GeoTIFFs.
       Filenames assumed pattern: tas_mean_C_cru_TS*_MM_YYYY.tif

  4. SNAP_SNOW_DIR  (or set USE_LITERATURE_SNOW=True to skip)
       Folder containing winter SWE rasters per year.

OUTPUT: training_table.csv with one row per (site, year, observation).
================================================================================
"""

